{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "seat_product_downgrade",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.68,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Highly on-target for the query: whether the promised seat product was delivered or downgraded (with/without notice) is a direct plausible driver of a 'no' recommendation, and gives a genuine grouping axis beyond the numeric Seat Comfort rating. Groundedness is mixed: 'qsuite_delivered' in sample 4 is explicitly warranted, and downgrade buckets appear reserved for rows that mention it. However 'flat_bed_delivered' functions as a default catch-all (samples 1,2,3,5) even where the text never states the seat type; distinguishing flat-bed vs QSuite when the source is silent is a soft inference rather than an extraction, and in sample 1 a malfunctioning seat is still labeled a clean delivery. Defensible domain inference overall but not fully evidenced."
    },
    {
      "name": "aircraft_age_and_condition",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.45,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Condition/age is a plausible-but-indirect axis for dissatisfaction, and nearly half the rows are NULL, weakening leverage for the query. Groundedness is the bigger problem: sample 1 asserts 'new_well_maintained' with no textual basis (and arguably contra the self-reclining broken seat), and sample 4 also asserts 'new_well_maintained' from a review that never discusses aircraft age or condition. Sample 3 ('very clean plane') and the correct 'Unknown' on silent rows (2,5) are fine, but the column confidently invents condition on silent rows, which is a hallucination pattern."
    },
    {
      "name": "cabin_crew_service_quality",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.62,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Service quality is central to recommendation outcomes, and the categorical scale (esp. the 'inconsistent' bucket) adds a modest new angle, though it substantially overlaps the original 'Staff Service' numeric column. Groundedness is strong: sample 2 ('good on my aisle / extremely slow where my wife was') maps well to 'inconsistent', samples 3-4 ('kind professional', 'nothing but praises') to 'excellent', sample 5 to 'good'. Sample 1's 'poor' is a slight inference from overall negativity rather than an explicit crew description, but it is defensible."
    },
    {
      "name": "food_beverage_quality",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.55,
      "source_groundedness": 0.5,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Relevant to the query but largely redundant with the existing 'Food & Beverages' rating, and 200 rows are NULL. Groundedness is mixed: samples 3 ('great tasting food') and 4 ('food outstanding, wine list outstanding') are well grounded and sample 5 correctly abstains ('Unknown'), but sample 1 outputs 'poor' and sample 2 outputs 'good' when neither review mentions food or drink at all — unsupported specifics where 'Unknown' was correct behaviour."
    },
    {
      "name": "service_consistency_across_legs",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.58,
      "source_groundedness": 0.55,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Consistency across legs is a reasonable analytical axis for multi-segment dissatisfaction, though it is partly derivable from the Route field. Groundedness is uneven: single-leg calls in samples 1-3 match the routes, but sample 5 ('New York to Johannesburg via Doha') is clearly multi-leg yet labeled 'single_leg', contradicting the route, and sample 4's uniformly praised trip is labeled 'variable_high_low' when the flights read as consistently high. Two of the multi-leg cases are mishandled."
    },
    {
      "name": "ffp_elite_status_handling",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.2,
      "source_groundedness": 0.82,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Near-constant boolean (571 false vs 21 true) that offers almost no discriminating leverage for explaining non-recommendations; effectively a rare-mention flag. Groundedness is acceptable: none of the sample reviews mention frequent-flyer/elite handling and all are correctly 'False', so the silent-row default behaves like a proper 'not stated'. Low usefulness rather than low grounding."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.56,
  "source_groundedness": 0.6,
  "rationale": "The set contains a couple of genuinely query-relevant axes — seat_product_downgrade (with/without-notice downgrades directly bear on why a flight is not recommended) and the 'inconsistent'/consistency framings of crew and cross-leg service. However usefulness is diluted: cabin_crew and food_beverage columns largely restate the existing Staff Service and Food & Beverages ratings, aircraft_age_and_condition is ~half NULL, and ffp_elite_status_handling is near-constant (21/592 true) with negligible leverage. On groundedness, correct extractions and appropriate 'Unknown' abstentions appear (samples 3-4, and several correct NULLs), but there is a recurring pattern of confidently filling silent rows with specific values: sample 1 asserts 'new_well_maintained' and food 'poor' with no basis, sample 4 asserts aircraft condition unsupported, and service_consistency mislabels a via-Doha itinerary as 'single_leg' and a uniformly praised trip as 'variable_high_low'. These are real hallucinations that pull the table-level grounding to a middling level."
}
